    "q-279199": {
      "dom_id": "step-q279199",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{13}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=13$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=13,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{13}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{13}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{13}=2\\sqrt{13}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
